	Announcing xmove 1.2, a pseudoserver for client
mobility. xmove 1.0 was released in January of 1994, and xmove
1.1b was released in May of 1994. This update includes, among
other changes, support for grey-scale servers and the ability to
place a client into suspended animation, whereby it is no longer
displayed anywhere, yet can be moved to any display at a later
date. This allows the client to survive the death of its server.

	xmove 1.2b has been updated to compile under linux
without modification.

	Unlike other pseudoservers which are designed to send an
application to multiple displays simultaneously, xmove is
designed to allow an application to be relocated between
displays. It requires no changes to your environment nor to your
configurations.

	As a pseudoserver, it sits between your display and your
application, listens in to the conversation, and tweaks bits as
necessary. If your computer is named chestnut, for example, you
typically set the DISPLAY environment variable to chestnut:0 in
order to start an application. When xmove is running, it creates
a display called chestnut:1. Anything sent to chestnut:1 will be
displayed on chestnut:0, but you will be able to move it to any
other networked computer.

	This is the third public release of xmove after over two
years work. Questions and requests can be sent either to me at
this address, or can be posted here. I try to keep up with
comp.windows.x, but do not always succeed, so if you post and
don't get a reply please follow up with email.

	And please remember that xmove is still relatively
untested, and as with all software products it won't be truly
stable until about the fourth release. 8) Also, with a
pseudoserver, no matter how many features you support, there are
always other configurations and applications that can cause
problems.

	Some of the main features of xmove are that it has
special support for clients which use the OpenLook standard,
including Drag-and-Drop. xmove also supports clients which use
custom colormaps, and clients which open multiple connections to
the server, such as FrameMaker.

	The source is available via anonymous ftp from
ftp.cs.columbia.edu:/pub/xmove as xmove.1.2.tar.gz. Please note
that it is compressed using gzip and you will need Gnu's gunzip
to uncompress it.

	Thanks for your interest,
	-- Ethan
