Cyclades is continuing to increase their support of Linux.  (The
next release may well be made by them directly. :-)  A new
version of the driver for the Cyclades Cyclom-Y asynchronous serial
multiplexer has been released.

  From the README:

  This document contains installation information for the Cyclades
  Cyclom-8Y 8-port and Cyclom-16Y serial extenders.  This version
  applies to kernel distribution 1.1.50 and other similar versions.
  (Reports have been received that they work with 1.1.64 and a change
  has been made to support 1.1.65, i.e. direct control of the higher
  signalling speeds.)

  ...

  This new driver is able to determine the irq used by a board,
  thus relieving the user from one more configuration hassle.
  (By default, the driver looks in four likely places for the
  board, so if you've got one, chances are it'll be found.)

  The setserial program, used to interrogate traditional serial
  ports, provides nearly the same amount of information about
  the Cyclom boards.  (The limit is due to the different IRQ
  handling, the lack of traditional UARTs, etc.)  The program
  can also be used to extend the range of signalling rates up
  to 115 Kbaud.

  ...

  The cyclades patches are available by ftp from sunsite.unc.edu under
  /pub/Linux/kernel/patch/serial as cyc.1.33.tgz.

  ...

  The Cyclom-Y models are:

  - Cyclom-8Ys  : 8 ports, RJ-12 connectors (6 wires, no RTS signal).
  - Cyclom-8Yb  : 8 ports, DB-25 connectors in a external box (full-modem).
  - Cyclom-8Yo  : 8 ports, DB-25 connectors in a octopus cable (full-modem).
  - Cyclom-16Y  : 16 ports, DB-25 connectors in a external box (full-modem).

  ...

  The setserial program can be used with the Cyclom ports to extend the
  range of communication speeds.  When the "spd_hi" option is set, the
  highest speed is mapped to 57600 bits/second and when the "spd_vhi"
  option is set, that speed is mapped to 115200 bits/second.



