Hi Linuxers,

I recently uploaded an improved version of my SAR set (Search-And-Rescue disk
maker) to sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/Incoming and tsx-11.mit.edu:/incoming.
The relevant files are: SAR-2.00.tar.gz and SAR.lsm. The primary site is
however (still) ftp.icce.rug.nl:/pub/unix.

I have no idea when the sysops of sunsite and tsx-11 will get to moving the
files to their "right" locations.

What is it?
===========

The SAR set contains a script (SAR-make), a couple of configuration files, and
postscript, sgml and plain ascii docs. The purpose is that it creates for you
a one- or two-floppy SAR set, which you can use in the event of disaster.
Disasters can be disks crashes, where you need to access your backup medium to
restore things, or losses of root passwords, where you need to gain access to
edit /etc/passwd on your hdu so that you can change the root password.

Basically, with SAR, you get to create customized root-login disks, with just
the stuff you need and with your newest kernel, modules and libs, with your
favorite editor and utilities, etc..

What are the changes?
=====================

Changes to the previous SAR versions are:
    -better configurability
    -SAR auto-searches for files to place on the SAR disks,
    -a more complete boot/login suite on the SAR disks, which should thereby
	avoid site-specific problems ("one size fits all"),
    -support for libs 4.6.20, which seem to need (w/u)tmp in /var/adm,
    -two-floppy setup supported (one for the kernel image, one for the rest),
	which therefore frees some 200K or more on the second disk,
    -better documentation, including a "quick start" section for knowledgable
	users,
    -and more, most of which are probably just details.

