 Someone pointed out that c.o.l.d is so huge that my post
 may have vanished, so here it is in c.o.l.a)

Since 1.1.58, the msdos filesystems has been enhanced to
take advantage of the more flexible buffer cache. The
performance gain was spectacular. In fact, for some user
it was even much more spectacular ... It was not working
at all :-(

Currently, the fs is not working on device with a large
sector size (1024 bytes). The msdos fs blindly assume
a block size of 512 bytes.

There is a good news and a bad news. The good one is that I
have modify it, removing all constant here and there. The
bad news is that I can't test it because I don't have such
hardware. I am trying to simulate this using
the new floppy utilities and formatting weird diskettes.
I am not sure it is a good simulation.

So I am looking for testers. Currently the new file system still
work correctly over a normal partition (512 bytes sectors). So

	-If you have such a device (I was told that some optical
	 disk behave like this).
	-If you have a mean to do reliable backups (in case :-( ).
	-And are ready to spend some time on this.

then you are a good candidate.

Just send me email. One thing that is still not done is the detection
of the proper condition forcing a switch from 512 bytes blocks to
1024 bytes (or whatever). Could you send me the output of a
mount command like this one (Using the old old msdos fs off course).
This will help me figure out the latest details.

mount -t msdos -o debug /dev/whatever /mnt

This goes generally in /usr/adm/messages if you are running klogd/syslogd

Hope to hear from some of you...


--------------------------------------------------------
Jacques Gelinas (jacques@solucorp.qc.ca)
